<p>Curiosity is the desire to know or understand something you don’t know yet.</p>
<p>It’s the feeling or drive that makes you ask “Why?”, “How?”, or “What happens if…?”</p>

<p>In simple terms:</p>
<p class="pipe-quote">Curiosity is the urge to explore the unknown.</p>

<p>It pushes us to:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ask questions</li>
  <li>Investigate things</li>
  <li>Learn new ideas</li>
  <li>Explore patterns and mysteries</li>
</ul>

<div></div>

<strong>Anyone Can Be Curious</strong>
<p>Curiosity does not require you to be highly intellectual or deeply passionate.</p>
<p>You can be curious about many things without being passionate about them or an expert in them.</p>
<p>Although passion may ignite or keep curiosity alive, it is not necessary for curiosity to exist.</p>

<p>At its core, curiosity is simply the ability and willingness to ask questions, even simple ones such as:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Why is the sky blue?</li>
  <li>Why is grass green?</li>
  <li>Why is water wet?</li>
</ul>

<p> Curiosity doesn’t require genius or deep passion — anyone can ask, explore, and wonder.</p>
<p>At its core, it is simply the willingness to ask questions and the desire to know.</p>
